The store (BUILD_RUNNER_STORE, on unless 0; the owner, N-412 p909: "no shared cache not each a build folder"; design
N-412 p925, rulings p927, conditions p928): each job runs in an empty place, $BUILD_RUNNER_HOME/places/pN, with its
worktree mounted at $BUILD_RUNNER_HOME/at/tree and the place at at/target in a user and mount namespace of its own
(no root), so every job's paths are the same whichever worktree it came from; its tests run inside that namespace.
Every rustc call goes through build-store-rustc, which looks the compile up in $BUILD_RUNNER_HOME/store by what it
reads (rustc's version, its arguments, cargo's variables, each extern crate's content, native libraries and the linker,
then the sources and env! values the compile's dep-info names) and links the stored outputs in (each checked against
its hash; a blob that differs is moved aside and the compile runs), else compiles and puts its outputs in. Build
script runs are looked up likewise (the script, its variables, what it said it reads). Nothing is kept in a place
between jobs: what is not in the store compiles. The end line counts the hits and misses; store/runs.jsonl has each
job's counts and the crates that missed; each entry records the job that put it (who, worktree, branch, commit,
train). The folder-per-profile mode below is BUILD_RUNNER_STORE=0.

What it does with each request (one build at a time in each shared folder, at most N at once across all folders, N
being the first line of $BUILD_RUNNER_HOME/parallel, read at each decision (else $BUILD_RUNNER_PARALLEL, else 4;
paper-research N-808: two, the owner's "we need quit time asap"), of them at most one fewer cold, so a warm request
always has a place; the next waits and is told why):
  - Warm: the folder was built last by this request's worktree, so only what changed since compiles (a named test, a
    check after an edit). Cold: never built, or last built by another worktree (status says "cold").
  - The shared folder is $BUILD_RUNNER_HOME/target/<profile> (debug, release, or --profile NAME), and
    target/<profile>-<triple> for a --target build (the Windows exe: release-x86_64-pc-windows-gnu). Every crate from the
    registry, and every path crate outside the worktree (rune, wgpu-hal), is compiled there once for all worktrees.
  - Our own crates (every path package whose manifest lies inside the caller's worktree) get the same artifact hash
    whichever worktree they come from, and cargo can take another worktree's build of them as fresh (16 Nov: the edge
    worktree's test linked main's docket_machine; 77d7d2d carried main's uncommitted client.rs; N-412 p11). So when the
    worktree differs from the one the folder last built, `cargo clean -p` takes each of our crates out of that folder
    first. A build from the same worktree is cargo's own business: its fingerprints follow the files there.
    Unless the switch can go by content (N-808): the folder records ($BUILD_RUNNER_HOME/built/) the content of every
    file of our crates it was built from; when our crates, every Cargo.toml, Cargo.lock and .cargo file are the same,
    and no crate of ours read a file outside that record (cargo's dep-info), the runner sets to now the times of the
    caller's files whose content differs (only their times: the content is not touched), and cargo rebuilds what
    reads them and keeps the rest.
  - cargo runs in a scope with MemoryMax=10G and MemorySwapMax=0, CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0, -j $BUILD_RUNNER_JOBS (4). No
    time limit: a job lasts while its caller is there to read it (the owner's rule of 14 Nov: no arbitrary time limits,
    a wait lasts while the awaited process lives; paper-research N-1446 p3253). A job whose caller has gone is stopped
    at once, when its connection ends, not at its next line: a caller here that ends, or on the build machine the
    relay of a caller whose link has gone (it ends when its ssh's input is silent for $BUILD_RUNNER_RELAY_SILENT_SEC,
    90 s, the caller saying a blank line each $BUILD_RUNNER_BEAT_SEC, 20 s, while it waits; a2h-speed N-1446 p3251).
  - A job that names a hold no longer running (its caller's link went, it was sent again) runs as an ordinary job,
    with a line saying so, instead of waiting for that hold for ever (p3251, eng-80's check at 01:23Z 25 Nov).
  - Below $BUILD_RUNNER_MIN_FREE_GB (12) GB free on the folder's disk a build is refused, with the free space said.
  - A shared folder over $BUILD_RUNNER_CAP_GB (20) GB is emptied before the next build in it, which is then cold:
    every other Cargo.lock or feature set adds registry builds that nothing else takes out (docket-core p29, 2-4).
  - Requests are served in the order they came (--front ones first), except that one for a free folder does not
    wait behind one whose folder is busy. Only the service's own user is served: a caller of another uid (a
    boxed agent's) is refused, since its build would run as the owner's user outside its box (paper-research p31).
  - If the caller goes away, its build is stopped (nobody reads it).
  - The queue as a page reads it: $BUILD_RUNNER_HOME/queue.json, written at each change (who, folder, verb, since,
    and for each waiting request why it waits; N-808, the releases page).
  - Where a job stands, as a page reads it (N-1334): the caller's side writes $BUILD_RUNNER_HOME/jobs/PID-WHEN.json, whole
    at each change: its agent and coord, worktree, branch and commit, verb and packages (never the command line), and
    its step: unreachable, sending, queued (how many ahead), compiling (K of M units, from cargo's own count),
    testing (the binary, K of N tests, how many failed), back, ended (rc, passed, failed). The service says the steps
    in the job's stream as lines the caller takes out again, so a job's output is what it was. A file whose pid is
    gone and whose step is not `ended` is a job cut with its caller.
